{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"castoff-tigerseye-ammonite","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nAn orphaned store write-lock froze the review-bot's startup on the Gerrit host for over an hour on\n2026-07-31, causing seven consecutive autodeploy failures (CloudWatch alarm `rebar-autodeploy-errors`,\n18:21 UTC onward). The lock could never be reclaimed, by design, and would not have self-healed.\n\n`src/rebar/_store/lock.py`:\n- `_owner_stamp()` (:152-154) writes `f\"{socket.gethostname()}:{os.getpid()}\"` into the lock dir.\n- `_mkdir_lock_is_stale()` (:171-190) returns `False` unless `host == socket.gethostname()` (:184).\n\nInside a container `socket.gethostname()` is the **container id**, and docker assigns a NEW one on\nevery `compose up -d` recreate. So a lock orphaned by the SAME host's previous container is\nclassified as a **foreign-host** lock — the shared-filesystem case the guard was written for — and is\nnever reclaimed. There is no age-based ceiling, so the orphan is permanent.\n\nThe conservatism itself is deliberate and correct in its intended setting; the docstring says \"never\nreclaim on anything short of proof (bug yaw-gravel-linen)\". The defect is that **hostname is not host\nidentity inside a container**, so the foreign-host branch misfires for what is actually the same host.\n\n## Observed (production, 2026-07-31)\n\n- Lock dir `/var/gerrit/site/reviewbot-tickets/.ticket-write.lock.d/`, created 18:30:49, owner stamp\n  `1e0fab75d5bd:3085`. Container `1e0fab75d5bd` ran 18:03:46-18:30:55 and was SIGKILLed mid-store-write\n  when a deploy recreated it (it logged a completed code_review artifact link and `Shutting down` in\n  the same second).\n- Every later container has a different id, so `_mkdir_lock_is_stale` returned False forever.\n- `run_ensures()` (`_store/ensures.py:158`) calls `write_lock()` with the defaults\n  `_DEFAULT_TIMEOUT=30` x `_DEFAULT_ATTEMPTS=2` = a 60s budget, then swallows `LockTimeout`. Startup\n  therefore took ~62s instead of <4s, exceeding autodeploy's `HEALTH_TIMEOUT=30`, so every deploy was\n  rolled back to `:prev`.\n- Journal evidence (spans 2026-07-03 -> 2026-07-31): 197 `is a writable rebar store` lines vs 183\n  `ensure env-id` lines = 14 lock-timeout boots, ALL on 2026-07-31 from 18:30:49 onward, zero in the\n  preceding four weeks. `.ensure-applied` remained stamped at the last successful sweep, 18:03.\n- Cleared manually by removing the orphaned lock dir; this ticket is the durable fix.\n\n## Approach\n\nMake the owner stamp stable across container recreates on one host (e.g. boot_id + pid, or a\nstart-time-qualified pid) so a same-host orphan is provably reclaimable, and/or add an age-based\nreclaim ceiling so a lock that cannot be proven live is reclaimable after a bounded interval. Keep\nthe existing refusal-without-proof behaviour for the genuine shared-filesystem case.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_store/lock.py` — `_owner_stamp`, `_mkdir_lock_is_stale`, `_reclaim_mkdir_lock`.\n- Tests for the container-recreate scenario and the shared-filesystem scenario.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A lock orphaned by a dead process on the SAME host is reclaimable even when the recorded\n      hostname differs because the process ran in a since-recreated container; verified by a test that\n      simulates a changed `gethostname()` with a dead pid.\n- [ ] A lock owned by a LIVE process is still never reclaimed, and a genuinely foreign-host lock is\n      still not reclaimed on the strength of hostname alone; verified by tests (no regression of\n      bug yaw-gravel-linen).\n- [ ] An unprovable lock does not block indefinitely: either an age ceiling applies or the failure is\n      surfaced rather than silently swallowed into a 60s stall.\n- [ ] make lint, make typecheck, and the default suite exit zero.","id":"304e-9c0f-2068-4d0e","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Store mkdir lock is permanently unreclaimable inside a container: gethostname() is the container id, so a same-host orphan looks foreign-host"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785527242729985001,"uuid":"beafa358-1dc5-439b-82ee-1374cc03dd00"}